<p>Cuba says its border and coast guard forces exchanged gunfire with a Florida-registered, US-flagged speedboat that entered Cuban territorial waters off the northern coast of Villa Clara province on February 25, leaving four people dead and six wounded. Cuban officials said the vessel ignored orders to identify itself, and that people aboard fired first—injuring the commander of a Cuban patrol craft—before Cuban forces returned fire and later evacuated the wounded for medical treatment. Havana described those on the boat as Cuban nationals living in the United States and said they were attempting an “infiltration” for “terrorist purposes,” alleging the group carried weapons and incendiaries, including assault rifles, handguns and Molotov cocktails. US officials said they were gathering their own information. Secretary of State Marco Rubio said the vessel was not a US government boat and that Washington would avoid speculation while checking whether any of those involved were US citizens or permanent residents, while Vice President JD Vance said the White House was monitoring the situation. The incident comes amid already tense US-Cuba relations. US Rep. Carlos Gimenez of Florida called for a federal probe, saying it raised serious concerns.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/7oHnDQ3vUMqQaetQ2u6VIHkIob5J2-8r_gAaZ902Rve51O6qR9nu2btBO7gi3y5jgJkPX2TJxAAnl8959RPXiqC08zeV6BRzAkCI_PDPJpHT82JvqtBs_E5IKZTriQ6rq74VbI_20r9lP6VCuKSOWFMKuKdzdBxbm7_XUl3SvvmTLdQkfhOsSxzWXeUsDIf8tgai3nbVBHAuzrjg7i4LoYcwp0hHqgViIkAStS6ypLHTrR0snU7U4S8-FbJKCqESBtCbWQYGf5DSW_ZuSg' alt='Cuban coast guard ships docked at the port of Havana on February 25, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Items recovered: Cuban authorities said the boat also carried tactical gear—ballistic vests, telescopic sights and camouflage uniforms—along with weapons and improvised incendiaries.</li><li>Location and registration: Cuban officials said they spotted the Florida-registered boat (FL7726SH) near Falcones Cay, off Villa Clara’s northern coast.</li><li>Prior clashes: The New York Times reported similar armed confrontations between US-registered vessels and Cuban border forces in 2022, in cases linked to migrant-smuggling operations.</li><li>US response details: Rubio called the incident “very unusual” and said US officials had not held direct discussions with Havana as Washington gathered its own information.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Cuba’s Interior Ministry: Authorities said the group consisted of Cuban nationals residing in the US and alleged the operation aimed at “infiltration” for “terrorist purposes,” with weapons and incendiaries on board. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c24drvj8yl2o'>BBC News</a>)</li><li>US government (State Department): Secretary of State Marco Rubio said the boat was not a US military or Coast Guard vessel and that Washington was gathering its own facts before reacting, including checking immigration or citizenship status of those involved. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/cuba-says-it-killed-4-people-aboard-florida-registered-speedboat-that-opened-fire-on-soldiers'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li><li>White House: Vice President JD Vance said the administration was monitoring the situation and expressed hope it was “not as bad as we fear it could be.”. (<a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/02/26/cuba-coast-guard-says-it-shot-and-killed-four-on-us-registered-speedboat_6750877_4.html'>Le Monde</a>)</li><li>Rep. Carlos Gimenez (Florida): Gimenez said the incident “raised serious concerns” and called for a federal investigation. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/cuba-says-four-killed-after-speedboat-from-florida-entered-cuban-waters-and-opened-fire'>The Straits Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/cuba-says-four-killed-after-speedboat-from-florida-entered-cuban-waters-and-opened-fire'>Cuba says it killed 4 exiles, wounded 6 others